# Q: can a player build a bridge, and if not, is he told why?
#
# Run against the default skirmish, which is the map the report came from:
#
#   MiVic.Game.exe --probe tools/probe/bridge.probe --probe-out artifacts/probe/bridge.txt
#
# The report: "I click on Γέφυρα and then on the water, and nothing happens." Nothing
# happening has three possible authors — the button never armed, the click never resolved to
# a cell, or the simulation refused the site — and a screenshot cannot tell them apart,
# because all three look exactly like a lake.
#
# The lake south-west of the player's base at (-248, -117) m has its shoreline at x = -262 m:
# the ground the base stands on is west of it, water east of it, and every water cell within
# ninety metres of that base is a site the simulation accepts.

# Frame the shoreline, so the pixels below are on screen and the shots are of the crossing.
focus -257 -210
zoom 170
pitch -0.90

# The simulation's own answer about a crossing here, and about two sites it refuses. A crossing is
# priced by its length — preparation plus so much a cell — so the two accepted spans below, seven
# cells and fifteen, do not cost the same, and a team that can afford one may not afford the other.
bridge -257 -210
bridge -210 -295
bridge -173 -257
bridge 0 0

# What pressing Γέφυρα does. This is the HUD's own path, not a flag set by the script: if the
# button cannot arm a bridge, this line is the bug and says so.
arm bridge

# The cursor over the water. `hover` reports the pixel a world point projects to and then what
# the client resolves that pixel back to, which is the round trip every click makes — and the
# cell it lands in, which over water has to be the water the player is looking at. The last of
# the three is half a metre inside the water's edge, where a march against the lake bed instead
# of the surface the player can see lands on the bank behind it and refuses a click on water.
hover -257 -210
hover -261 -210
hover -261.5 -210

# The ghost of the crossing, coloured by whether the site would be taken.
hud on
shot artifacts/probe/bridge-ghost.png

# A site the simulation refuses: the water at (-173, -257) m is 25 cells across, wider than any
# span. The ghost goes red and the panel beside the button says which rule refused it.
hover -173 -257
click
shot artifacts/probe/bridge-refused.png

# The player's click on the water, through the same path a mouse release takes.
hover -257 -210
click

# Nothing is across yet: the order has been recorded and paid for, and the work has not started.
bridges
tick 2
bridges

# Two cells in. The deck is a stamp of the span from one bank, `attributes` shows the ford
# following it, and the panel carries the line the player reads while they wait.
tick 90
bridges
attributes -257 -210
shot artifacts/probe/bridge-building.png

# And whole: every cell of the span is ford, and every cell of it is deck.
tick 160
bridges
attributes -257 -210
attributes -257 -201

# The crossing itself, with the panel out of the way.
hud off
shot artifacts/probe/bridge-built.png

# And down it comes: enemy salvos on the middle of the span. A bridge is a chain of blocks, so this
# does not delete the crossing — it knocks blocks out, the water comes back through the hole, and
# what is left either side of it is deck that leads nowhere. One salvo is not enough for a block
# (200 hit points against 95 a salvo); the second leaves it at 10, and the third takes it.
blast -257 -210 22 95 2
bridges
blast -257 -210 22 95 2
bridges
blast -257 -210 22 95 2
bridges
attributes -257 -210
attributes -257 -201

# The hole in the deck, from the same camera as the whole crossing above.
shot artifacts/probe/bridge-cut.png

# A cut crossing is repaired with the order that built it: the gap is water again, so the site is a
# site, and the price is the price of the span the simulation measures from there.
bridge -257 -210

# The round trip over water is exact, which is the regression guard on the projection: marched
# against the bed instead of the surface the player sees, this lands metres away.
expect "a click on water resolves to the water it was aimed at" 0.0 0.0 0.3
